  <main><section id="activity-1"><h1 id="activity-1-h">Activity 1<span class="kicker">Teacher assessment identity reflection</span></h1>
<div class="card"><p>1. Are your ideas about assessment the same now?</p>
<p>My main idea has not changed. I still believe assessment is about job
readiness, because if students cannot apply their knowledge in the real
world, a certificate is just a piece of paper. This belief comes from my
graduate diploma in National University of Singapore, which prepared me
to work as an IT professional. What has changed is that I now see
assessment as a chance for students to show their skills, not as
pressure.</p>
<p>2. Do you think assessment has the same purpose and intentions as it
did back then?</p>
<p>Partly. When I was a student, assessment felt mainly like a test to
pass and a grade to earn. Now I see it has two purposes: supporting
learning and certifying achievement (Looney et al., 2018). In VET, it
must also show that students can perform to workplace standards. After
completing my PhD, I also realised job readiness includes research
careers, not only industry.</p>
<p>3. To what extent have your attitudes to assessment changed since
becoming an educator?</p>
<p>Quite a lot. When I started teaching, I added a lot of content so
students could learn everything I had learned from my studies and work.
Now I design assessment that meets the learning outcomes and is
realistic to complete on time. As a full-time lecturer who is also
studying, I understand that my students are also balancing study, work
and personal commitments.</p>
<p>4. Do you believe your attitude is influenced by your own
experience?</p>
<p>Yes, strongly. At school I always wanted my submissions to be
perfect. At university in Singapore, a stressful lecturer made me lose
confidence, while a supportive lecturer helped me achieve better
results. This matches Looney et al. (2018), who note that teachers'
beliefs about assessment are shaped more by their own experience of
being assessed than by theory. This is why I design assessments to
reduce student anxiety.</p>
<p>5. What other elements may influence your beliefs about
assessment?</p>
<p>My confidence in the subject matters. I feel confident teaching and
assessing software engineering, but less so in networking, where I have
no industry experience. As Looney et al. (2018) observe, a teacher's
assessment identity is shaped by their professional context,
institutional policies and collegial relationships. In my setting, this
is reflected through VET principles, training packages, CQU policies and
peer interactions. Finally, my role changes. Depending on each student's
ability, I am a teacher, guide or coach, but I am also an assessor who
must judge competence fairly.</p></div>

