</section><section id="activity-3"><h1 id="activity-3-h">Activity 3<span class="kicker">Comparing ChatGPT outputs</span></h1>
<p><strong>Chat GPT response</strong></p>
<p><img alt="Screenshot" loading="lazy" src="data:image/jpeg;base64,"/><img alt="Screenshot" loading="lazy" src="data:image/jpeg;base64,"/><img alt="Screenshot" loading="lazy" src="data:image/jpeg;base64,"/></p>
<p>My prompt ("How to use Socratic questioning") was different from the
professor's ("Write an essay on how to use Socratic questioning"), and
this changed the output. The professor's ChatGPT 3.5 response followed
an essay format, with a title, headings, numbered lists and formal
phrases such as "delve into". My response gave a practical, step-by-step
explanation in paragraphs, with a concrete example ("Social media is
harmful") and sample questions for each step. However, the core ideas
were very similar: clarifying meaning, probing assumptions, examining
evidence, considering other perspectives and questioning conclusions.
Neither response cited sources, so the information cannot be verified.
This shows that prompt wording strongly shapes AI output, and that AI
produces different wording even on the same topic. Students could
therefore submit AI-generated work with similar ideas but different
words, which plagiarism-detection tools may not identify.</p>
<p>In my networking units, students could paste open-book quiz questions
into AI, or use it to write the Stage 1 equipment justifications, the
Stage 2 network plan and the Stage 4 documentation. However, AI cannot
build and configure the network in our lab. Stage 3 is observed, the
scenario is specific (Lopez Law, 10.30.40.x addressing), and students
must explain their own configuration and troubleshoot a fault in front
of me. These make AI misuse much easier to detect, and they protect the
authenticity of the evidence.</p>
