<td>Q15-Q16</td>
<td>Stages 3-4 + introduced fault/log</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>8. Document implemented network</td>
<td>-</td>
<td>Stage 4</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Support and quality assurance</strong></p>
<p><u>Formative practice</u> will be provided through compulsory weekly
labs, including fault-finding, and optional weekly quizzes. These
activities will prepare students for the summative tasks but will not be
graded.</p>
<p><u>Reasonable adjustments</u> may change how evidence is collected,
but not the competency standard. Support services and an approved
support person will remain available.</p>
<p>Before future delivery, a colleague will <u>review</u> the revised
quiz, templates, model answers and observation checklist to support
reliable assessment decisions (ASQA, 2025).</p></div>
</section><section id="activity-7"><h1 id="activity-7-h">Activity 7<span class="kicker">Moderation checklist</span></h1>
<div class="card"><p><strong>Moderation Checklist</strong></p>
<p><strong>Unit cluster</strong>: Please see <a href="#unit-cluster">"Unit cluster"</a> section.</p>
<p>Moderation is a review of assessment to ensure it is appropriate,
understandable, consistent, fair and transparent from development to
final results (Flinders University, n.d.). Assessment integrity also
requires students to be judged on their own work against clear,
consistent standards (Sadler, 2009). As I am often the sole assessor on
my campus, I work with my Rockhampton co-coordinator and involve
students in understanding assessment criteria, as recommended for sole
markers (Flinders University, n.d.).</p>
<p>The checklist builds on our <strong>existing</strong> practice and
adds three improvements <strong>I propose</strong>: an Assessment
Marking Guide (AMG) for every unit, a student version of the AMG, and an
independent VET review when an assessment is updated without a scheduled
validation panel. As we run four intakes each year, assessments may
require updates between intakes; independent review ensures changes are
checked before delivery rather than waiting until the next validation
panel.</p>
<p><strong>Pre-assessment</strong></p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>

<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th><strong>#</strong></th>
<th><strong>Moderation step</strong></th>
<th><strong>Why (source)</strong></th>
<th><strong>Status</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>1</td>
<td>Check tasks, instructions and marking tools align with unit
requirements and weekly labs.</td>
<td>Validity (Flinders University, n.d.)</td>
<td>Existing</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td>2</td>
<td>Create an AMG (objectives, criteria and sample answers) for each
unit without one.</td>
<td>Shared, explicit standards (Sadler, 2009)</td>
<td>Proposed</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
