</section><section id="activity-8"><h1 id="activity-8-h">Activity 8<span class="kicker">Proposed rubrics</span></h1>
<p><strong>Units:</strong> Please see <a href="#unit-cluster">"Unit
cluster"</a> section.</p>
<p>To my knowledge, our VET-ICT units do not currently have clear
rubrics, assessment relies on the quiz result and the observation
checklist. The two rubrics below are my <strong>proposal</strong>, to be
used with the student and teacher versions of the Assessment Marking
Guide. Because assessment is competency-based, there are no marks or
weightings: the result is <strong>Satisfactory</strong> only when every
criterion is at least Satisfactory. The “Exceeds” level is
<strong>feedback only</strong> and does not change the result.</p>
<p><strong>Rubric 1 (holistic): Small Business Networking Quiz (Task
1)</strong></p>
<p><strong>Task:</strong> 18-question open-book online quiz covering
SOHO network knowledge. Most questions are auto-marked against an answer
key, the two short-answer troubleshooting questions (Q15–Q16) are
teacher-marked. This rubric describes the quiz response <strong>as a
whole</strong>.</p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>

<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th><strong>Level</strong></th>
<th><strong>Description of the quiz response as a whole</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Not yet satisfactory</strong></td>
<td>One or more answers are incorrect/incomplete, showing gaps in client
requirements, standards, devices/services, TCP/IP/OSI, network types,
installation, security, diagnostics or media. Fault diagnosis/fix is
incorrect, incomplete or not in the student’s own words.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Satisfactory</strong></td>
<td>All 18 answers are correct, covering required networking knowledge.
Short answers correctly diagnose faults from ‘ipconfig’ output and
explain practical, correctly sequenced fixes in the student’s own
words.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Exceeds (feedback only)</strong></td>
<td>As for Satisfactory, plus explains why the fault caused the output,
how to verify the fix (e.g. ping the gateway), and how to prevent
recurrence.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><em><strong>Result</strong></em>: Satisfactory or Exceeds =
Satisfactory (100% correct). Not yet satisfactory = the student studies
the areas missed and reattempts (within the five attempts allowed).</p>
<p><strong>Rubric 2 (analytic): SOHO Network Project – all four stages
(Task 2)</strong></p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>

<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th><strong>Criterion</strong></th>
<th><strong>Not yet satisfactory</strong></th>
<th><strong>Satisfactory</strong></th>
<th><strong>Exceeds (feedback only)</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>1. Clarify requirements and research solutions (Stage
1)</strong></td>
<td>Requirements not clarified with the client (i.e., equipment,
cabling, NBN plan or costs missing or unsuitable; choices not
justified).</td>
<td>Clarifies requirements orally, recommends suitable devices, cabling,
supplier and business NBN plan with correct costing and total, justifies
each item against a client requirement.</td>
<td>Also compares alternatives and explains trade-offs (cost,
performance, scalability).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>2. Plan the network (Stage 2)</strong></td>
<td>Plan is incomplete or IP conflicts or addresses outside
10.30.40.x.</td>
<td>Complete technical plan: logical diagram, IP and naming plan
(10.30.40.x, DHCP range, fixed IPs), WiFi settings, file and print
sharing, and an efficient task sequence with minimum disruption.</td>
<td>Also labels the diagram with addresses and connection types and
allows for future growth.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>3. Implement and configure the network (Stage
3)</strong></td>
<td>One or more required devices not set up or not working as planned
(see observation checklist).</td>
<td>Sets up the modem, PC, NAS, shared printer, VoIP phone, two WiFi
devices, extender and camera as per the plan, all observation checklist
items signed off.</td>
<td>Also configures devices efficiently with minimal assistance and
explains any necessary changes to the plan.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>4. Secure the network (Stages 2–3)</strong></td>
<td>Security settings missing or insecure (e.g. weak WiFi security);
unauthorised device not blocked.</td>
<td>Applies WPA2/WPA3 WiFi security, MAC filtering that blocks an
unauthorised device, and user accounts and permissions matched to staff
roles.</td>
<td>Also addresses client-data confidentiality (e.g. VPN and camera
access controls, least privilege).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>5. Test and troubleshoot (Stages 2–4)</strong></td>
<td>Tests not run or not recorded, the introduced fault is not found or
fixed; cannot explain own configuration.</td>
<td>Runs the planned tests (ping, ipconfig, nslookup, tracert) and
records results; finds and fixes the introduced fault, clearly explains
own configuration when questioned.</td>
<td>Also explains the cause of the fault at protocol level and how to
prevent it.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>6. Document the implemented network (Stage 4)</strong></td>
<td>Documentation does not match the implemented network, screenshots,
test results or troubleshooting log missing.</td>
<td>Updates the plan to match the implementation, with configuration
screenshots, test results and a troubleshooting log, in a professional
technical format.</td>
<td>Also records the reasons for changes so another technician could
maintain the network.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><em><strong>Result</strong></em>: Satisfactory only if all six
criteria are at least Satisfactory. Any “Not yet satisfactory” criterion
= feedback and resubmission of that stage (within the three attempts
allowed).</p>
<p><strong><u>Reflection</u></strong>: I prefer the analytic rubric for
most VET-ICT tasks. Our units currently use an observation checklist and
quiz results, with no clear rubric, so students often cannot see what
“satisfactory” looks like until they receive feedback. The analytic
rubric breaks the project into six criteria across all four stages, so
both students and I can see exactly which part is not yet satisfactory
and what to fix before resubmission. It also supports self-checking and
the student version of the Assessment Marking Guide, developing
students’ evaluative judgement (Tai et al., 2018), and makes judgements
more consistent between me and my Rockhampton co-coordinator. Although
VET results are only Satisfactory or Not Satisfactory, I included an
“Exceeds” level for feedback only, to encourage deeper work without
changing the competency decision. The holistic rubric suits the quiz,
where most questions are auto-marked and one overall judgement of
knowledge is quick and consistent. However, it gives limited feedback,
so a student who is not yet satisfactory may not know which knowledge
area to revise. Overall, the analytic rubric takes longer to write, but
it is fairer and more transparent for students (Sadler, 2009).</p>
</section><section id="activity-9"><h1 id="activity-9-h">Activity 9<span class="kicker">Evaluating feedback approaches</span></h1>
<p><strong>Unit cluster</strong>: Please see "Unit cluster" section.</p>
<p>Feedback aims to close the gap between where students are and where
they need to be (Hattie &amp; Timperley, 2007), but it only works if
students can understand it and act on it (Molloy &amp; Boud, 2013). The
table compares three feedback approaches, drawing on research and on how
I already use each one in my units.</p>
<p><strong><br/>
</strong></p>
<p><strong>Comparison of written, audio and video feedback</strong></p>
<div class="tablewrap"><table>

<thead>
<tr class="header">
<th></th>
<th><strong>Written</strong></th>
<th><strong>Audio / verbal</strong></th>
<th><strong>Video</strong></th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr class="odd">
<td>How I use it now</td>
<td>Moodle forum/Q&amp;A, Teams posts with guiding questions on each
assessment (purpose, benefits, expectations and assessment support),
individual Teams messages when needed, quiz feedback.</td>
<td>Verbal feedback after weekly labs and office phone calls about
student progress, guided by Moodle activity (materials viewed,
activities completed and progress), especially in Weeks 1–4.</td>
<td>Lab demonstration videos for students who need support or make
mistakes when setting up network devices.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Cost</strong></td>
<td>Low: uses existing Moodle and Teams.</td>
<td>Low: office phone or Teams if recorded (Lunt &amp; Curran,
2010).</td>
<td>Low to moderate: screen-recording software (e.g., Zoom), a headset,
and storage space for large files (McCarthy, 2015).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Time implications</strong></td>
<td>About 20 minutes per student for detailed summative feedback
(McCarthy, 2015), short forum answers can reach the whole class at
once.</td>
<td>Fastest to produce: about 10–15 minutes per student (McCarthy,
2015). Live calls need to be scheduled and can take longer.</td>
<td>Slowest at first: about 20–25 minutes per student including
recording and uploading (McCarthy, 2015), but one demonstration video
can be reused by many students and intakes.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Affordances</strong></td>
<td>Permanent, “official” record, links directly to criteria and the
rubric, easy to re-read, accessible without special devices (McCarthy,
2015).</td>
<td>Tone and emphasis make it more personal and supportive, students are
far more likely to open audio feedback than collect written feedback
(Lunt &amp; Curran, 2010). Live calls allow two-way dialogue (Molloy
&amp; Boud, 2013).</td>
<td>Shows exactly what to do, which suits practical, visual tasks,
students find it clear, personal and motivating, and most prefer it
(Henderson &amp; Phillips, 2015; McCarthy, 2015).</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>Limitations</strong></td>
<td>Can be brief or seen as less detailed, tone can be misread, long
comments can overwhelm, students may not read or act on it (Molloy &amp;
Boud, 2013).</td>
<td>No visual element, phone calls leave no record unless notes are
kept, recorded audio may need several listens (McCarthy, 2015) and a
transcript for accessibility.</td>
<td>Large files, hard to match comments to a specific part of a document
(Henderson &amp; Phillips, 2015), mainly one-way, with limited evidence
of improved learning outcomes (Mahoney et al., 2019), needs captions for
accessibility.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="even">
<td><strong>Best suited for</strong></td>
<td>Specific corrections to assessment documents and checklists,
criteria-based and record-keeping feedback.</td>
<td>Explaining overall strengths, issues, diffiulties and improvement
strategies, early progress conversations.</td>
<td>Demonstrating practical networking tasks, e.g. device configuration
and troubleshooting.</td>
</tr>
<tr class="odd">
<td><strong>What I can incorporate</strong></td>
<td>Keep written feedback for records, link comments to the analytic
rubric.</td>
<td>Keep progress calls and record brief notes, add Teams voice messages
for quick individual feedback on lab work.</td>
<td>Maintain demonstration videos, build a library of common
configuration mistakes, and trial short screen-recorded feedback for the
Stage 2 network plan.</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Reflection</strong>: what I will incorporate</p>
<p>No single format is best, so I will combine them according to
purpose, and add captions or transcripts where needed for accessibility.
Written feedback will be used for specific corrections to assessment
documents and checklists, while audio or video feedback will be used
where further explanation or demonstration is beneficial. Written
feedback remains essential for formal assessment sign-offs and
assessment records, because it links clearly to the criteria. Audio,
especially my verbal feedback in labs and phone calls in weeks 1–4
prompted by Moodle activity data, is the best way to reach students
early, show care and allow real dialogue while there is still time to
act, which builds their confidence. Video is particularly suitable for
practical networking tasks because I can demonstrate configuration or
troubleshooting processes visually, my demonstration videos are also
sustainable, as they can be reused across intakes. Because video is
mostly one-way (Mahoney et al., 2019), I will pair it with dialogue
through Q&amp;A sessions and “explain your work” questions, so that
students act on the feedback rather than just receive it (Molloy &amp;
Boud, 2013).</p>
